WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.270 --> 00:00:01.500
DALE MARKOWITZ: Hi, I'm Dale.

00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:02.730
This is Making With ML.

00:00:02.730 --> 00:00:04.080
And today, I want to
talk about something

00:00:04.080 --> 00:00:05.850
you've probably
experienced if you've ever

00:00:05.850 --> 00:00:06.870
been on the internet.

00:00:06.870 --> 00:00:09.360
I'm talking about user-generated
content that violates

00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:10.770
a platform's terms of service.

00:00:10.770 --> 00:00:13.085
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:15.870 --> 00:00:18.060
Almost all content
platforms, whether they're

00:00:18.060 --> 00:00:21.660
YouTube, or Twitter, or OKCupid,
or "The New York Times",

00:00:21.660 --> 00:00:24.375
have some strategy for
dealing with bad user input.

00:00:24.375 --> 00:00:26.250
And this is called their
moderation strategy.

00:00:26.250 --> 00:00:28.860
Now, you can imagine
what's OK to post on Reddit

00:00:28.860 --> 00:00:31.320
or a dating app might not
fly on "The New York Times",

00:00:31.320 --> 00:00:34.050
so these platforms have to
consider what is allowed

00:00:34.050 --> 00:00:35.530
under their terms of service.

00:00:35.530 --> 00:00:37.080
These platform
maintainers also have

00:00:37.080 --> 00:00:40.312
to consider the implementation
of their moderation strategy.

00:00:40.312 --> 00:00:41.520
Let me give you some options.

00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:44.640
Option number one-- you could
hire a team of human moderators

00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:46.830
to go through every piece
of user-submitted content

00:00:46.830 --> 00:00:49.170
before it's posted to OK it.

00:00:49.170 --> 00:00:50.748
This is called pre-moderation.

00:00:50.748 --> 00:00:53.040
And as you can imagine, it's
really good at making sure

00:00:53.040 --> 00:00:54.630
that nothing bad slips
through the cracks,

00:00:54.630 --> 00:00:55.740
because human
beings are actually

00:00:55.740 --> 00:00:57.270
looking at what's being posted.

00:00:57.270 --> 00:00:59.080
But it also doesn't
scale very well,

00:00:59.080 --> 00:01:00.690
and it takes a large
human moderation

00:01:00.690 --> 00:01:03.460
team that scales with the
size of your user input.

00:01:03.460 --> 00:01:06.000
So you'd only use it if it's
extremely, extremely important

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:07.920
that no bad bits get
through the cracks.

00:01:07.920 --> 00:01:10.440
Option number two-- you
could ask your users

00:01:10.440 --> 00:01:13.650
to flag bad content for you and
then send this flagged content

00:01:13.650 --> 00:01:16.170
to a human moderation
team for later review,

00:01:16.170 --> 00:01:17.900
maybe in a sort
of priority queue

00:01:17.900 --> 00:01:20.460
where they figure out what's
important to review first.

00:01:20.460 --> 00:01:22.620
This is called post-moderation,
because the content

00:01:22.620 --> 00:01:24.870
is moderated after it's
already been posted.

00:01:24.870 --> 00:01:28.230
For example, when you're served
a YouTube video or a Google ad

00:01:28.230 --> 00:01:30.510
and you have the option
to report that content,

00:01:30.510 --> 00:01:33.030
you're seeing a
post-moderation strategy.

00:01:33.030 --> 00:01:34.620
The advantage to
doing this is that it

00:01:34.620 --> 00:01:37.050
allows your human
moderation team to scale.

00:01:37.050 --> 00:01:38.533
But the disadvantage,
of course, is

00:01:38.533 --> 00:01:40.950
that then your users have to
actually see this potentially

00:01:40.950 --> 00:01:42.280
offensive content.

00:01:42.280 --> 00:01:44.640
And in some instances,
this would be unacceptable.

00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:46.723
Both of these approaches
have major drawbacks.

00:01:46.723 --> 00:01:49.140
You're either having your human
moderation team go through

00:01:49.140 --> 00:01:51.930
every single piece of
content before it's posted,

00:01:51.930 --> 00:01:54.600
or you're potentially subjecting
your users to content that

00:01:54.600 --> 00:01:56.670
should have been
flagged but that wasn't.

00:01:56.670 --> 00:01:59.370
And neither of these approaches
work for real-time moderation,

00:01:59.370 --> 00:02:01.740
like for example, if we're
doing a video stream or chat

00:02:01.740 --> 00:02:04.620
messages, which brings us
to option number three--

00:02:04.620 --> 00:02:07.392
AI-powered moderation, which
is what we're building today.

00:02:07.392 --> 00:02:08.850
The idea is to use
machine learning

00:02:08.850 --> 00:02:12.960
to analyze content like, text,
speech, images, video, and flag

00:02:12.960 --> 00:02:15.250
the content the instant
that it's created.

00:02:15.250 --> 00:02:17.940
You could then send that content
to a team of human moderators

00:02:17.940 --> 00:02:19.720
so that your users
never have to see it,

00:02:19.720 --> 00:02:21.810
but your moderation
team can scale better.

00:02:21.810 --> 00:02:23.250
Now you might
wonder, why not use

00:02:23.250 --> 00:02:26.440
AI to just moderate your
entire moderation solution?

00:02:26.440 --> 00:02:27.945
So let me add a disclaimer here.

00:02:27.945 --> 00:02:30.540
Machine learning models
are always probabilistic,

00:02:30.540 --> 00:02:33.048
which means that they
invariably make mistakes.

00:02:33.048 --> 00:02:34.590
And sometimes they're
biased, meaning

00:02:34.590 --> 00:02:37.260
that they make mistakes in
particularly bad, consistent,

00:02:37.260 --> 00:02:39.540
and potentially
embarrassing ways.

00:02:39.540 --> 00:02:42.240
So I always recommend
using AI in conjunction

00:02:42.240 --> 00:02:44.730
with human beings, having
this human in the loop.

00:02:44.730 --> 00:02:47.280
For example, you might have
your algorithms put comments

00:02:47.280 --> 00:02:49.170
into a moderation queue
for your moderators

00:02:49.170 --> 00:02:51.930
to review, or allow
users to appeal decisions

00:02:51.930 --> 00:02:53.640
that were made by the AI.

00:02:53.640 --> 00:02:55.350
So knowing that,
today I'll show you

00:02:55.350 --> 00:02:58.470
how to make a simple AI-powered
moderation bot for the chat

00:02:58.470 --> 00:03:00.060
platform, Discord.

00:03:00.060 --> 00:03:01.050
Let's get started.

00:03:01.050 --> 00:03:02.633
Today, we're going
to build a bot that

00:03:02.633 --> 00:03:05.760
sits in a Discord server
and analyzes user messages.

00:03:05.760 --> 00:03:07.780
When the bot detects
a message is toxic,

00:03:07.780 --> 00:03:09.840
it will flag it with
a dynamite emoji.

00:03:09.840 --> 00:03:11.400
It'll also keep
track of insulting,

00:03:11.400 --> 00:03:14.190
flirtatious, spammy, and
nonsensical messages and flag

00:03:14.190 --> 00:03:16.110
them with an appropriate emoji.

00:03:16.110 --> 00:03:18.660
It'll keep a scoreboard
of emojis or karma

00:03:18.660 --> 00:03:20.010
for each user in the chat room.

00:03:20.010 --> 00:03:22.330
And if a user types too
many toxic messages,

00:03:22.330 --> 00:03:23.713
they'll get kicked
from the room.

00:03:23.713 --> 00:03:26.130
Now, don't worry if you've
never done any machine learning

00:03:26.130 --> 00:03:27.480
before because,
for this project,

00:03:27.480 --> 00:03:28.897
we're going to use
the Perspective

00:03:28.897 --> 00:03:31.620
API to do the complicated
machine learning bits for us.

00:03:31.620 --> 00:03:33.810
It takes in strings of
text and produces scores

00:03:33.810 --> 00:03:37.110
for different attributes,
like toxicity, insultingness,

00:03:37.110 --> 00:03:39.930
explicitness, and
even just incoherence.

00:03:39.930 --> 00:03:41.880
To create these models,
the Perspective team

00:03:41.880 --> 00:03:43.680
use millions of comments
from data sources,

00:03:43.680 --> 00:03:45.720
like the New York
Times, Wikipedia,

00:03:45.720 --> 00:03:47.225
and many other open data sets.

00:03:47.225 --> 00:03:48.600
If you want to
see how they built

00:03:48.600 --> 00:03:50.320
the model and the
architecture behind it

00:03:50.320 --> 00:03:51.930
and even learn how
to train your own,

00:03:51.930 --> 00:03:54.223
check out the
Perspective API website.

00:03:54.223 --> 00:03:55.890
To use Perspective,
you need to fill out

00:03:55.890 --> 00:03:59.063
a quick Google form explaining
how you plan to use the API.

00:03:59.063 --> 00:04:00.480
Once your project
is approved, you

00:04:00.480 --> 00:04:02.320
should be able to
use it in your app.

00:04:02.320 --> 00:04:05.520
Next, go ahead and download
the Making with ML GitHub repo

00:04:05.520 --> 00:04:08.220
and navigate to the
Discord moderator folder.

00:04:08.220 --> 00:04:10.830
Here's all the code you'll
need to run your bot.

00:04:10.830 --> 00:04:13.020
The file,
Perspective.js shows you

00:04:13.020 --> 00:04:15.501
how to use the Perspective
API in JavaScript.

00:04:15.501 --> 00:04:17.459
At the top, you can see
all the different types

00:04:17.459 --> 00:04:19.320
of attributes the
tool recognizes,

00:04:19.320 --> 00:04:22.290
like insult, toxicity,
spam, incoherence,

00:04:22.290 --> 00:04:24.750
flirtation, and more.

00:04:24.750 --> 00:04:26.370
Now, let's connect
the Perspective API

00:04:26.370 --> 00:04:27.583
to a Discord bot.

00:04:27.583 --> 00:04:30.000
To do that, you'll need to
sign up for a Discord developer

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:31.030
account.

00:04:31.030 --> 00:04:33.480
Once you have an account,
create a new application.

00:04:33.480 --> 00:04:35.130
Then create a new
bot by clicking

00:04:35.130 --> 00:04:37.230
on Bot in the left-hand panel.

00:04:37.230 --> 00:04:43.050
Give your bot an avatar and
a cute name, and you're set.

00:04:43.050 --> 00:04:44.940
The brain of our Discord
bot lives entirely

00:04:44.940 --> 00:04:47.190
in the file Discord.js.

00:04:47.190 --> 00:04:49.350
Here at the top, you
can see our emoji map,

00:04:49.350 --> 00:04:52.440
which tells our bot how to react
to different user comments.

00:04:52.440 --> 00:04:55.770
Flirtations get a kissy
face, and spam gets a fish.

00:04:55.770 --> 00:04:57.540
Down in the Evaluate
Message function,

00:04:57.540 --> 00:04:59.340
we call the
Perspective analyzeText

00:04:59.340 --> 00:05:02.040
function to detect
attributes in the message.

00:05:02.040 --> 00:05:04.950
If an attribute is found,
we react with an emoji.

00:05:04.950 --> 00:05:07.180
And if the user has sent
too many toxic messages,

00:05:07.180 --> 00:05:09.595
we temporarily kick
them from the channel.

00:05:09.595 --> 00:05:11.970
The next step is to run your
Discord bot from the command

00:05:11.970 --> 00:05:12.930
line.

00:05:12.930 --> 00:05:13.430
Awesome.

00:05:13.430 --> 00:05:15.455
Now, it's ready to be
added to your server.

00:05:15.455 --> 00:05:17.870
In the Discord developer
panel under OAuth,

00:05:17.870 --> 00:05:19.670
select the permission
your bot needs--

00:05:19.670 --> 00:05:23.060
sending messages, adding
reactions, and kicking users.

00:05:23.060 --> 00:05:24.730
Copy the URL above
into your browser.

00:05:24.730 --> 00:05:28.530
And from here, you'll be able
to add your bot to a server.

00:05:28.530 --> 00:05:30.740
Now if we go to our
Discord server, boom.

00:05:30.740 --> 00:05:32.360
The moderator bot
is in the channel,

00:05:32.360 --> 00:05:35.810
keeping track of everything I
type and reacting with emojis.

00:05:35.810 --> 00:05:38.510
If you want to build this
Discord moderator by yourself,

00:05:38.510 --> 00:05:39.740
the code is in GitHub.

00:05:39.740 --> 00:05:42.110
And you can follow along
in my very detailed blog

00:05:42.110 --> 00:05:44.010
post in the description.

00:05:44.010 --> 00:05:45.080
And there you go.

00:05:45.080 --> 00:05:47.630
Now you have your very
own AI-powered chat bot

00:05:47.630 --> 00:05:49.340
moderator for Discord.

00:05:49.340 --> 00:05:51.530
Now, the moderation
strategy that our bot used

00:05:51.530 --> 00:05:52.310
is very simple.

00:05:52.310 --> 00:05:53.810
It just kicks users
from the channel

00:05:53.810 --> 00:05:56.618
when they say too many things
the AI thinks is toxic.

00:05:56.618 --> 00:05:58.160
But like we discussed
earlier, that's

00:05:58.160 --> 00:05:59.535
actually not a
very good strategy

00:05:59.535 --> 00:06:01.380
for a real in-production
moderation,

00:06:01.380 --> 00:06:03.240
because the model
can make mistakes.

00:06:03.240 --> 00:06:06.290
In fact, the Perspective website
specifically says, this API

00:06:06.290 --> 00:06:09.970
is not designed for fully
automated moderation solutions.

00:06:09.970 --> 00:06:12.140
And they even list a
bunch of known biases

00:06:12.140 --> 00:06:13.550
that the model has.

00:06:13.550 --> 00:06:14.920
Just think of it this way.

00:06:14.920 --> 00:06:16.620
AI is just one
piece of the puzzle,

00:06:16.620 --> 00:06:19.250
and it works better when human
partners are in the loop.

00:06:19.250 --> 00:06:20.180
That's all for now.

00:06:20.180 --> 00:06:22.263
If you want to learn more
about moderation and AI,

00:06:22.263 --> 00:06:24.110
make sure you check
out the blog below.

00:06:24.110 --> 00:06:25.985
And let me know what
problems you want to see

00:06:25.985 --> 00:06:28.100
solved with AI in
the comments below.

00:06:28.100 --> 00:06:31.190
But remember, those
comments are moderated, too.

00:06:31.190 --> 00:06:32.190
That's all for now.

00:06:32.190 --> 00:06:34.000
See you next time.

